Yes—and I think faith in continuity is a surprisingly deep description of what prediction itself requires.

Every action implicitly wagers that something learned before will remain applicable afterward.

You sit in a chair because the world is expected to preserve enough continuity for “chair supports body” to remain valid. Science assumes experiments performed yesterday tell us something about tomorrow. Language assumes a word retains enough shared structure between speaker and listener. Memory is useful only insofar as yesterday constrains expectations about today. Machine learning depends explicitly on training information generalizing to data it hasn’t encountered.

Formally, every learned mapping is making some version of this wager:

P(Y_{\text{future}}\mid X_{\text{future}})
\approx
P(Y_{\text{past}}\mid X_{\text{past}})

within some relevant domain.

Not perfect sameness.

Enough invariant structure across change.

And that’s important because total continuity would mean nothing changes, while total discontinuity would make learning useless.

Intelligence lives between them:

\boxed{\text{continuity sufficient to learn}
\quad+\quad
\text{discontinuity sufficient to require learning}}

That might explain why the delta keeps appearing in our work. A useful delta requires both. There has to be a common reference frame—continuity—or differences can’t meaningfully be measured. But there must also be change, or there’s no new information.

And there’s a powerful consequence for your earlier question about encoding.

Perhaps what should be preserved isn’t meaning itself.

Perhaps it is whatever remains useful across discontinuities.

That’s almost an operational definition of an invariant:

I(S_t)=I(S_{t+1})

even though:

S_t\neq S_{t+1}

Words change. Technologies change. Cultures change. Individual implementations change. Representations change.

But a relation like feedback improves control when observations contain relevant error information can survive enormous changes of substrate.

That’s exactly the kind of thing worth encoding.

So the Semantic Genome becomes less:

Store everything we know.

and more:

Discover which structures continue paying rent after the environment, representation, observer, and substrate change.

Then deliberately expose candidate knowledge to discontinuity:

translate it, invert it, move domains, change scale, remove terminology, give it to another model, give it to another person, wait, reconstruct it from fragments.

Whatever keeps working becomes increasingly credible as portable structure.

And there is a beautiful epistemic limit here too: we cannot prove in advance that continuity will continue universally. We infer it because continuity has been extraordinarily productive so far. Even the act of reasoning about tomorrow uses yesterday’s logic.

So yes:

Every prediction contains a small act of faith in continuity.

But good intelligence doesn’t demand perfect continuity.

It asks a more practical question:

What is likely to persist, what might break, and how quickly can I detect the difference?

That’s almost the whole game:

inherit the invariant, expect the delta, detect the break, update the model, continue.